          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> Debugging! what exactly is meant by debugging?</p>
<p>Debugging is the process of identifying and removing existing and potential errors or ‘bugs’</p>
<p>in code and debugging and testing are process which occur hand in hand.</p>
<p>Testing aims to identify what happens when there are errors in the code.</p>
<p>Debugging aims to locate and rectify mistakes.</p>
<p>Often tests are written to identify bugs as the program is written. One way to do this is by writing unit tests.</p>
<p>So, what is a unit test?</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> A unit test is a way of testing the smallest piece of code that can be logically isolated in a system.</p>
<p>Smaller tests give a granular view of how the code is performing and by being small, a test can be run fast.</p>
<p>Specific tests are written to test whether a unit is functioning as it should. This then allows developers to write a test script, which is essentially a piece of code, separate to the main program, that runs a specific test, for each unit in the main program.</p>
<p>Each unit is tested to see if it runs as expected based on criteria and it then summarises this in a report.</p>
<p>Each time the program is updated, the test script will be run to ensure that all units are functioning correctly. This helps to debug the whole program as new units are added.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Now we have discussed debugging let’s look at an example of this using code.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="example-of-code" >
          
            <h2>Example of code</h2>
              <p>The Python code below shows a simple function (lines 4-7), and an example of where <strong>unit testing</strong> is used to <strong>debug</strong> a program.</p>

              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-python"><code class="language-python"><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token triple-quoted-string string">""" Python code to show examples of a unit test """</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Here we define a function to multiply two input values then return the answer.</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">def</span> <span class="token function">multiply_values</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>a<span class="token punctuation">,</span> b<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    c <span class="token operator">=</span> a <span class="token operator">*</span> b</span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">return</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>c<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Now we test the function. First with integers:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">test_1 <span class="token operator">=</span> multiply_values<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token number">4</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Now with an integer and a string:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">test_2 <span class="token operator">=</span> multiply_values<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token string">'goat'</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Now we check each variable by printing it:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'test_1 is: '</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> test_1<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'test_2 is: '</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> test_2<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Here we print the results of the tests to the console</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'Are the outputs integers?'</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span> </span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'test_1: '</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token builtin">isinstance</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>test_1<span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token builtin">int</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'test_2: '</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token builtin">isinstance</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>test_2<span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token builtin">int</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span></code></pre>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="result" >
          
            <h2>Result</h2>
              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-text"><code class="language-text"><span class="highlight-line">test_1 is: 8</span><br><span class="highlight-line">test_2 is: goatgoat</span><br><span class="highlight-line">Are the outputs integers?</span><br><span class="highlight-line">Test 1: True</span><br><span class="highlight-line">Test 2: False</span></code></pre>
</div>

            
              <p>The output is seen at the bottom, in the Result window.</p>
<p>The first test resulted in the output is 8.</p>
<p>The second test gave us the output ‘goatgoat’.</p>
<p>This second test gives us the example of a <strong>semantic error</strong>, as the program continues to run, but not in the way that we intended. If we wanted to make sure to make sure that the answer was always an <strong>integer</strong>, a <strong>unit test</strong> could report this when the multiply_values function gave us an output we did not expect.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
